course: dictionary
lesson: 10618
title: Fair and unfair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unfair means not fair. It is when people are not treated equally, when someone does not follow the rules, or when someone cheats or takes more than their share.
explain: You will hear this word a lot in games and at school. «That's unfair!» is a common complaint when one person gets more than their turn. Good friends try to share fairly and take turns.
explain: In maths, unfair sharing means groups are not equal. A fair dice gives each number the same chance, but an unfair (loaded) dice does not.
explain: In stories, characters often fight against unfair rules or unfair treatment. Look at the words around unfair to see what the author means.
fact: Unfair means not fair: not treating people equally, in a right and honest way, or not following the rules.
fact: «Unfair» is an adjective, and it can change to show more or less: more unfair, most unfair.
fact: A fair dice gives each number the same chance; an unfair (loaded) dice does not.
fact: Saying someone cheated without proof is unfair; if nobody saw it, drop it.
fact: Blaming others for your mistake is unfair, and people stop trusting you.
exercise: «Ava took the biggest slice and gave her sister the smallest.» Why is this unfair? | Because cake is a dessert | Because the sharing is not equal | *Because Ava is taller than her sister | Because the cake was yummy
exercise: A fair dice gives each number the same chance. What does an unfair (loaded) dice do? | *Some numbers come up more often than others | It always lands on six | It cannot be rolled at all | Every number has the same chance
exercise: Find and copy the word that means not fair in this sentence: «Saying someone cheated without proof is unfair; if nobody saw it, drop it.» | nobody | proof | cheated | *unfair
test: «That's unfair!» is a common complaint. What does a complaint like this usually mean? | You have just won the game | You are very happy about a prize | *You think something is not right or not fair
test: «Blaming others for your mistake is unfair, and people stop trusting you.» What happens when someone does this? | Their mistake disappears | People give them more chances | Everyone gives them a prize | *People stop trusting them
exam: Which choice is fair when you share with a friend? | *Take turns and share equally | Take the biggest share first | Keep the best toy for yourself | Hide the sweets so nobody else gets any
